4169921875, 0.01113128662109375, -0.0153961181640625, -0.0185394287109375, -0.0210113525390625, 0.022369384765625, -0.024322509765625, -0.0031833648681640625, 0.00870513916015625, 0.002105712890625, 0.01236724853515625, 0.00036263465881347656, 0.0021839141845703125, 0.0016736984252929688, -0.003353118896484375, -0.00847625732421875, 0.005084991455078125, -0.007656097412109375, 0.0169830322265625, 0.00786590576171875, -0.0248260498046875, -0.0027217864990234375, 0.005825042724609375, -0.0078277587890625, 0.02044677734375, 0.01253509521484375, 0.00730133056640625, 0.037353515625, -0.0098724365234375, 0.0288543701171875, -0.0018491744995117188, -0.0013017654418945312, -0.006282806396484375, 0.014739990234375, -0.01541900634765625, 0.00928497314453125, 0.017669677734375, -0.0247802734375, 0.004486083984375, -0.016021728515625, -0.02288818359375, 0.018463134765625, 0.00891876220703125, 0.01404571533203125, -0.05340576171875, 0.03509521484375, -0.0135040283203125, -0.0166778564453125, -0.00125885009765625, 0.01241302490234375, 0.0018186569213867188, 0.0022335052490234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Definitions**\n\nWe investigate the relationship between weak convergence of synaptic input measures and the firing behavior of sparse threshold-reset networks in the fast-synapse limit. Our analysis requires precise definitions and model assumptions, which we enumerate below.\n\n**1.1. Notation and Signed Measures**\n\nLet $\\mathcal{M}(\\mathbb{R})$ denote the space of finite signed Borel measures on $\\mathbb{R}$. For $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{M}(\\mathbb{R})$, we write $\\mu = \\mu^+ - \\mu^-$, where $\\mu^+, \\mu^-$ are finite nonnegative Borel measures (the Jordan decomposition). For a sequence $(\\mu_N^+, \\mu_N^-)$ of pairs of finite nonnegative Borel measures, we say $(\\mu_N^+, \\mu_N^-)$ converges *componentwise weakly* to $(\\mu^+, \\mu^-)$ if, for every bounded continuous function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$,\n\\[\n\\int f\\,d\\mu_N^\\pm \\to \\int f\\,d\\mu^\\pm \\quad \\text{as } N\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThis definition extends to signed measures by linearity.\n\n**1.2. Fast-Synapse Limit**\n\nLet $K_\\varepsilon \\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$ be a family of nonnegative, compactly supported functions with $\\operatorname{supp}(K_\\varepsilon) \\subset [0,\\varepsilon]$ and $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. The *fast-synapse limit* refers to the regime $\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$, i.e., all synaptic kernel delays vanish. This is a singular limit: as $\\varepsilon \\to 0$, $K_\\varepsilon$ converges weakly to the Dirac delta $\\delta_0$, but the order of arrival of inputs may not be preserved under weak convergence.\n\n**1.3. Causal Event Protocol and Model Assumptions**\n\n- *Spike Processing*: Spikes are processed in strict temporal order. After a spike, the membrane potential is clamped at reset value for a fixed refractory period $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}>0$.\n- *Synaptic Kernels*: All kernels $K_\\varepsilon$ are $C^\\infty$, nonnegative, compactly supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$, and satisfy $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. All synaptic delays are strictly positive.\n- *Dale Compatibility*: Each neuron is either purely excitatory or purely inhibitory.\n- *Typewise-Mixing*: Let $G_N$ be a random directed graph on $N$ vertices, partitioned into finitely many synaptic classes. For each class, the induced subgraph is such that, for any fixed neuron, the empirical distribution of incoming edges from that class converges in total variation to the class distribution as $N\\to\\infty$.\n\nThese assumptions are necessary to ensure well-posedness of the dynamics, to avoid pathological instantaneous feedback, and to model realistic biological constraints (see [3,4]).\n\n**2. Model Specification and Spike Train Construction**\n\nWe consider a leaky integrate-and-fire neuron with membrane potential $V(t)$ governed by\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = -\\lambda (V(t) - V_{\\mathrm{rest}}) + I_{\\mathrm{syn}}(t),\n\\]\nwhere $\\lambda\\geq 0$ is the leak rate, $V_{\\mathrm{rest}}$ is the resting potential, and $I_{\\mathrm{syn}}(t)$ is the total synaptic input. When $V(t)$ reaches threshold $\\theta$, the neuron emits a spike, $V$ is reset to $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$, and held at $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$ for $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$.\n\nWe construct two families of input spike trains for a target neuron, parameterized by a small delay $\\varepsilon>0$ and pulse masses $a>0$ (excitatory), $b>0$ (inhibitory):\n\n- *Excitatory-first*: The neuron receives an excitatory spike at $t=0$ and an inhibitory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n- *Inhibitory-first*: The neuron receives an inhibitory spike at $t=0$ and an excitatory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n\nEach spike is modeled as $a K_\\varepsilon(t)$ (excitatory) or $-b K_\\varepsilon(t-\\delta)$ (inhibitory, with $\\delta=0$ or $\\varepsilon$). As $\\varepsilon\\downarrow 0$, both input measures converge weakly to $a\\delta_0$ and $-b\\delta_0$.\n\n**3. Main Results: Firing Behavior and Robustness**\n\nWe now state and prove the main result for a single neuron.\n\n**Theorem 1 (Order Sensitivity in the Fast-Synapse Limit).**  \nLet $K_\\varepsilon \\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$ be nonnegative, supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$, and $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. Let $a, b > 0$, $\\varepsilon > 0$, and let $V(t)$ solve\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = -\\lambda (V(t) - V_{\\mathrm{rest}}) + a K_\\varepsilon(t) - b K_\\varepsilon(t-\\delta)\n\\]\nwith $V(0) = x$, threshold $\\theta$, and reset protocol as above. Consider two cases:\n- (i) $\\delta = \\varepsilon$ (excitatory-first: excitation at $t=0$, inhibition at $t=\\varepsilon$),\n- (ii) $\\delta = 0$ (inhibitory-first: inhibition at $t=0$, excitation at $t=\\varepsilon$).\n\nAssume $\\varepsilon < \\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, $\\lambda \\geq 0$, and $V_{\\mathrm{rest}}, V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$ arbitrary. Then, in the limit $\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$,\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0} \\mathbb{I}\\{\\text{neuron fires in (i)}\\} = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\lim_{\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0} \\mathbb{I}\\{\\text{neuron fires in (ii)}\\} = 0\n\\]\nif and only if $x + a - b < \\theta \\leq x + a$.\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet us analyze the two cases:\n\n- *Excitatory-first*: For $t \\in [0, \\varepsilon)$, $V(t)$ increases due to the excitatory input. Since $K_\\varepsilon$ is supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$ and integrates to $1$, the total excitatory increment is $a$. If $x + a \\geq \\theta$, $V(t)$ crosses threshold before $t = \\varepsilon$. After firing, $V$ is reset and clamped for $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}} > \\varepsilon$, so the subsequent inhibitory input has no effect. If $x + a - b < \\theta \\leq x + a$, the neuron fires in this case.\n\n- *Inhibitory-first*: The inhibitory input arrives at $t=0$, reducing $V$ by $b$ before any excitation. The subsequent excitatory input at $t=\\varepsilon$ increases $V$ by $a$, but the total is $x - b + a$. If $x + a - b < \\theta \\leq x + a$, then $x - b + a < \\theta$, so $V$ never reaches threshold.\n\nThe \u201cif and only if\u201d follows from the above. The limit as $\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$ is justified by the smoothness and support of $K_\\varepsilon$, ensuring that the increments occur in the correct order and that the threshold crossing is determined by the sum of increments.\n\n**Robustness.**  \nSuppose $|x - x'| \\leq \\eta$, $|a - a'| \\leq \\eta$, $|b - b'| \\leq \\eta$, and $|dV/dt| \\leq \\eta$ (bounded drift). If $\\theta$ is at least $2\\eta$ away from $x + a - b$ and $x + a$, then the firing outcome is unchanged by continuity of the solution with respect to initial data and parameters.\n\n**4. Network Construction and Propagation to Sparse Networks**\n\nLet $G_N$ be a random directed block graph with $N$ neurons, each with $q_N$ outgoing edges ($q_N\\to\\infty$, $q_N/N\\to 0$), partitioned into finitely many synaptic classes. Each neuron is either purely excitatory or inhibitory (Dale compatibility). The network is initialized identically in both the excitatory-first and inhibitory-first protocols, differing only in the order of paired spikes as above.\n\n**Propagation Mechanism and Quantitative Analysis.**  \nFor each neuron, the probability that the excitatory spike arrives before the inhibitory one is $1/2$ in the limit, due to random edge assignments and independent delays. Let $F_N^{(E)}$ and $F_N^{(I)}$ be the total firing counts in the excitatory-first and inhibitory-first systems. By linearity of expectation and independence"}}